<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document" style="font-size:144%;">Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ravi Vakil
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Dept. of Mathematics, Stanford University, Stanford CA 94305–2125
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:rvakil@stanford.edu">rvakil@stanford.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sameera Vemulapalli
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Dept. of Mathematics, Harvard University, Cambridge MA 02138–2901
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:vemulapalli@math.harvard.edu">vemulapalli@math.harvard.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: September 5, 2025.</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A degree <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> genus <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> cover of the complex projective line by a smooth curve <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> yields a vector bundle on the projective line by pushforward of the structure sheaf. Which bundles are possible? Equivalently, which <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles over <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contain such covers? (In the language of many previous papers: what are the scrollar invariants of the cover?)</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We give a complete answer in degree <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, which exhibits the expected pathologies. We describe a polytope (one per degree) which we propose gives the complete answer for primitive covers, i.e. covers that don’t factor through a subcover. We show that all such bundles (for primitive covers) lie in this polytope, and that a “positive proportion” of the polytope arises from smooth covers. Moreover, we show the necessity of the primitivity assumption. Finally, we show that the image of the map from the Hurwitz space of smooth covers to the space of bundles is not preserved by generization (for <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g\gg_{d}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg_{d}1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">1991 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary 14H60, Secondary 14H51, 14H30. 
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2410.22531v4/weirdo2.jpeg" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" width="338" height="250" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The geography of degree <math id="S0.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="S0.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely (see §<a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the locus <math id="S0.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\;\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\;\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to tetragonal covers and their cubic resolvents: a three-dimensional locus (mostly primitive) and a two-dimensional locus (all imprimitive). Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is a projection of Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> corresponds to a point of Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is a subset of Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
