<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a genus <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> cover of the complex projective line by a smooth irreducible complex curve (<math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>).
Then <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> canonically embeds in a <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, any such genus <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> cover (fiberwise scheme-theoretically nondegenerately) embedded in a <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle must be canonically embedded. Moreover, the <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle in which <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> embeds is precisely the projectivization of the Tschirnhausen bundle of <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, which we introduce below. This paper deals with the question: <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which bundles arise as Tschirnhausen bundles</span>? By the discussion above, we see that this is equivalent to the question: <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles over <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contain smooth genus <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> covers</span>?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">1.1. 
Let <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\coloneq(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}/{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\coloneq(\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}/{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> (the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tschirnhausen bundle of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>).
The trace map <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, when multiplied by <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, splits the exact sequence</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.Ex1.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="22.57" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-11.478591397439722px" version="1.1" viewBox="7.61 11.48 268.20 22.57" width="268.20"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 7.610350076103501 22.57285217732764) translate(0,3.46) translate(7.61,0)"><g transform="translate(-7.61,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{0\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{0\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 7.610371189690138 -0.27673578005007266 L 7.610371189690138 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(40.82,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 7.610371189690138 -0.27673578005007266 L 40.819150408191504 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 40.819150408191504 -0.27673578005007266 L 40.819150408191504 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(40.82,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.76em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.28em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.32" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="24.29"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 73.41310198382584 -0.27673578005007266 L 73.41310198382584 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(106.62,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 73.41310198382584 -0.27673578005007266 L 106.62188120232722 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 106.62188120232722 -0.27673578005007266 L 106.62188120232722 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(106.62,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.48em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="34.32"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 149.24161939250163 -0.27673578005007266 L 149.24161939250163 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(182.45,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 149.24161939250163 -0.27673578005007266 L 182.450398611003 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 182.450398611003 -0.27673578005007266 L 182.450398611003 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(182.45,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.27em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.48" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="17.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 208.31794152867502 -0.27673578005007266 L 208.31794152867502 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(241.53,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 208.31794152867502 -0.27673578005007266 L 241.5267207471764 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 241.5267207471764 -0.27673578005007266 L 241.5267207471764 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(241.53,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{0,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{0,}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">so we have <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\overset{\sim}{\longleftrightarrow}{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟷</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\overset{\sim}{\longleftrightarrow}{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Vector bundles on <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> split completely (uniquely up to non-unique isomorphism), so <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\cong{\mathscr{O}}(e_{1})\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\cong{\mathscr{O}}(e_{1})\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> nondecreasing.
We call the integers <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">scrollar invariants of the cover</span>. (Caution: in a few sources, the definition of “scrollar invariants” differs from these by <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.)
From <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C,{\mathscr{O}}_{C})=h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C,{\mathscr{O}}_{C})=h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so the <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are all positive. From <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C,{\mathscr{O}}_{C})=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C,{\mathscr{O}}_{C})=g</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}e_{i}=d+g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{d-1}e_{i}=d+g-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the canonical embedding in question is <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\coloneq\operatorname{Proj}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\operatorname{Sym}^{\bullet}{\mathscr{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\coloneq\operatorname{Proj}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\operatorname{Sym}^{\bullet}{\mathscr{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. (See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">LVaW</a>, §3]</cite> for details.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">1.2.
<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Central Question.</span>  <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What can the bundle <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> (or equivalently, the Tschirnhausen bundle) be? Equivalently: What are the possible <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math>? If <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stack of smooth degree <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> genus <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> covers, <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stack of vector bundles on <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the “Tschirnhausen morphism”</span></p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tsch</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is defined by <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\pi]\mapsto{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\pi]\mapsto{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, what is the image of <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The answer to this question when <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> is given in §<a href="#S2" title="2. Warm-up: Low degree (
        
          
            ≤
            d
            3
          
        
      ) and low genus (
        
          
            ≤
            
              +
              
                ⁢
                2
                g
              
              1
            
            d
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and the case <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits the subtleties that should be expected in general.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For general <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, the second author proposes an answer to Central Question <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> when <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive.
(In keeping with the language for number fields, we say that <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">primitive</span> cover if <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> does not factor nontrivially through an intermediate smooth cover <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, if <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, or if the monodromy group of <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the full symmetric group <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily primitive.)
Define the polytope</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}:=\{(\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1})\;:\;\sum\overline{e}_{i}=1,\;0\leq\overline{e}_{1}\leq\cdots\leq\overline{e}_{d-1},\text{ and }\overline{e}_{i+j}\leq\overline{e}_{i}+\overline{e}_{j}\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">:</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> and </mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}:=\{(\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1})\;:\;\sum\overline{e}_{i}=1,\;0\leq\overline{e}_{1}\leq\cdots\leq\overline{e}_{d-1},\text{ and }\overline{e}_{i+j}\leq\overline{e}_{i}+\overline{e}_{j}\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">1.3.
<span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture (Vemulapalli). —</span>  <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, the possible scrollar invariants for primitive degree <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> genus <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> smooth covers are precisely those that, scaled down by <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+g-1</annotation></semantics></math>, lie in <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The cases <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> are verified in §<a href="#S2" title="2. Warm-up: Low degree (
        
          
            ≤
            d
            3
          
        
      ) and low genus (
        
          
            ≤
            
              +
              
                ⁢
                2
                g
              
              1
            
            d
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
In §<a href="#S4" title="4. Scrollar invariants of primitive covers lie in the polytope 
        
          
            
            P
            d ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we show that the scrollar invariants of every primitive smooth cover
must lie in <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">1.4.
<span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem. —</span>  <span id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a primitive degree <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> morphism from a genus <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curve. Then the tuple of scrollar invariants of <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, scaled by <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+g-1</annotation></semantics></math>, lies in the polytope <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We first remark that the set of limit points of the scrollar invariants of primitive (not necessarily smooth) covers, scaled down to lie in <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, is all of <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>; this follows from an appropriate combination of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ve1</a>, Thm. 1.1.7, Thm 1.1.9]</cite>. Thus the difficulty of proving Conjecture <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> lies in showing the existence of <em id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smooth</em> curves with prescribed scrollar invariants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Now observe that if a smooth cover <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has scrollar invariants not lying in <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then it <span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">must</span> factor through <span id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">some</span> intermediate cover! This is one additional way in which the scrollar invariants contain a good deal of information about the cover <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We further conjecture that the answer to Question <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in general (not requiring primitivity) is that for each <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a finite union of rational polytopes <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, all of dimension <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-2</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property that the possible scrollar invariants are precisely those which lie in <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> when scaled down by <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+g-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{e}_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{e}_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The example of <math id="S1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) shows that <math id="S1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> need not equal <math id="S1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the answer to Central Question <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for all smooth covers may, and in fact is expected to, be different from the answer to Central Question <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for primitive covers if <math id="S1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not prime! (From <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ve2</a>, Thm. 1.46]</cite>, one can extract an expectation of <math id="S1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>, or the product of two primes, or a prime power. A precise conjecture for general <math id="S1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> seems intractable, however.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, if <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, Conjecture <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implies that the image of <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex (interpreted as a subset of <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>). In §<a href="#S5" title="5. The possible scrollar invariants do not form a convex set if 
        
          d
        
       is not a prime power ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we show that the image of <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math> is not convex for <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=6</annotation></semantics></math> and sufficiently large genus (so <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=6</annotation></semantics></math> is the first “nonconvex degree”, if Conjecture <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds for <math id="S1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5</annotation></semantics></math>), and we indicate how to show the analogous statement for any <math id="S1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> that is not a prime power.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">In §<a href="#S6" title="6. All concave scrollar invariants (a positive proportion of the polytope 
        
          
            
            P
            d
          
        
      ) are achieved ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we show that a “positive proportion” of the polytope <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is actually achieved. More precisely,
we show the following.
We say a sequence of real numbers <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{0},\dots,x_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{0},\dots,x_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">concave</span> if <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i+1}-x_{i}\leq x_{i}-x_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i+1}-x_{i}\leq x_{i}-x_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">1.5.
<span id="S1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem. —</span> 

<span id="S1.p14.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Choose integers <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1},0)</annotation></semantics></math> is a concave sequence.
Then there is a primitive cover <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by a smooth (irreducible) curve with <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\cong\oplus_{i=1}^{d-1}{\mathscr{O}}(e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}\cong\oplus_{i=1}^{d-1}{\mathscr{O}}(e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We produce curves with “concave” scrollar invariants by constructing certain singular curves on Hirzebruch surfaces. The crucial input is Wood’s explicit and striking parametrization of curves on Hirzebruch surfaces using binary forms. (Wood’s work builds on a long history of associating rings to binary forms, including <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">N</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">DF</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BiM</a>]</cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">DDS</a>]</cite>.) Wood’s construction gives us enough control over the algebra to control the scrollar invariants of the normalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">1.6.
<span id="S1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remarks.</span>  (a)
The “concave locus” in <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (where the sequence <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> is nondecreasing and <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1},0)</annotation></semantics></math> is a concave sequence) is a full-dimensional subpolytope of <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. (For example, in <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it is the “upper half triangle” that is the convex hull of <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/6,1/3,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/6,1/3,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/5,2/5,2/5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/5,2/5,2/5)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/3,1/3,1/3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/3,1/3,1/3)</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">(b) But note further that the <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are not required to be nondecreasing, so by considering the <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> produced by this theorem, and reordering them to be nondecreasing, we get a union of full-dimensional polytopes in <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that are shown to be realizable by our construction. (For example, in <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, by taking <math id="S1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}\geq e_{3}\geq e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}\geq e_{3}\geq e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and then reordering, we obtain the “right half triangle” that is the convex hull of <math id="S1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/5,2/5,2/5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/5,2/5,2/5)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/3,1/3,1/3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/3,1/3,1/3)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/4,1/4,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/4,1/4,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>; see Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. So together with the previous remark, this theorem shows that three quarters of <math id="S1.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is “realizable”.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">(c) The number of such polytopes is <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>: Given an ordered list, there are <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> ways of rearranging the list elements so they are monotonically increasing then monotonically decreasing, and Theorem <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the existence of covers in each of these cases. Reversing the sequence gives the same construction. (The case <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> will make this combinatorial argument clear to the reader.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">(d) A variation of Theorem <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be used to get the final quarter of <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> (omitted because all of <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is already shown to be achieved in §<a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), but the authors do not see how to get all of <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in general. See Remark <a href="#S6" title="6. All concave scrollar invariants (a positive proportion of the polytope 
        
          
            
            P
            d
          
        
      ) are achieved ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Question <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> has been considered in a number of geometric contexts. We mention here other work showing that scrollar invariants in <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are achieved. The main theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Co1</a>]</cite> shows that a particular type of scrollar invariant is achieved, and is the first result of this sort of which we are aware for general degree <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">LL</a>, Ex. 1.3.7]</cite> also describes an interesting case. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">CaVZ</a>, §7]</cite> shows that certain highly imbalanced scrollar invariants are achieved, albeit not for smooth curves.
Most dramatically, the main theorems of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bal</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Co2</a>]</cite> (a simpler second proof of the result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bal</a>]</cite>), and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP</a>]</cite> can be interpreted as showing that in some asymptotic sense, all points “near” the most “generic” or “balanced” point of <math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (where the <math id="S1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal) are achieved, and these include cases not covered by what we show in §<a href="#S6" title="6. All concave scrollar invariants (a positive proportion of the polytope 
        
          
            
            P
            d
          
        
      ) are achieved ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. (We did not check to see if <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP</a>]</cite> gives a region of <math id="S1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of positive volume, because the statement of their result does not immediately translate in this way. The results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bal</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Co2</a>]</cite> do not give regions of positive volume.) See also the discussion of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">KO</a>]</cite> in §<a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">In §<a href="#S7" title="7. The image of 
        
          
            :
            Tsch
            
              →
              
                ⁢
                H
                u
                
                  
                  r
                  
                    
                      
                      
                      
                    
                    
                      d
                      ,
                      g
                    
                  
                
              
              
                ⁢
                B
                u
                
                  
                  n
                  
                    
                    P
                    1
                  
                
              
            
          
        
       is not preserved under generization (
        
          d
          &gt;
          4
          ,
          g
          ≫
          
            d
          
          1
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> we ask if the image of the morphism <math id="S1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tsch</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> (of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)) is preserved by generization, i.e. if <math id="S1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Tschirnhausen bundle of a smooth cover, and <math id="S1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generization of <math id="S1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is it true that <math id="S1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">also</em> the Tschirnhausen bundle of a smooth cover? There are a number of reasons to hope the image of <math id="S1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved by generization. If so, then the simple deformation theory of <math id="S1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> would allow us to prove the existence of curves with other scrollar invariants. It would also allow us to prove statements about fibers of <math id="S1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math> by looking at the most degenerate cases, which are far more tractable (the “minimal scrollar invariants” correspond to curves on a Hirzebruch surface not meeting the directrix). The fact that the image of <math id="S1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math> seems to be of such a nice form (conjecturally a polytope in the primitive case) might further give hope of good behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">And indeed the image of <math id="S1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved by generization when <math id="S1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+1\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+1\leq d</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Surprisingly, we show (in Proposition <a href="#S7" title="7. The image of 
        
          
            :
            Tsch
            
              →
              
                ⁢
                H
                u
                
                  
                  r
                  
                    
                      
                      
                      
                    
                    
                      d
                      ,
                      g
                    
                  
                
              
              
                ⁢
                B
                u
                
                  
                  n
                  
                    
                    P
                    1
                  
                
              
            
          
        
       is not preserved under generization (
        
          d
          &gt;
          4
          ,
          g
          ≫
          
            d
          
          1
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) that the image of <math id="S1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tsch</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not preserved by generization (<math id="S1.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p22.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g\gg_{d}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg_{d}1</annotation></semantics></math>). In the case where <math id="S1.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not a prime power, one might expect this from the nonconvexity of the image of <math id="S1.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tsch</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}</annotation></semantics></math>, but when <math id="S1.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a prime power, we expect the image to be convex and yet it is not preserved by generization. In the primitive case, we even show that the image of the restriction <math id="S1.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}^{p}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}^{p}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Tsch</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}^{p}:\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}^{p}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> to primitive covers is not preserved by generization (<math id="S1.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p22.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g\gg_{d}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg_{d}1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Question <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is related to questions about the lattice structure of rings of integers in number fields (see §<a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>); scrollar invariants are naturally analogous, when appropriately scaled, to successive minima of rings of integers. However, we may ask a more refined question: we are interested in not just <em id="S1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> Tschirnhausen bundles occur, but also how often they occur, and this question has a number theoretic analogue as well. In §<a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> we discuss how the latter question can be described via a conjectural density function on <math id="S1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in both the arithmetic and geometric settings. We then further speculate that these density functions extend to the Grothendieck ring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p24" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p24.1" class="ltx_p">1.7.
<span id="S1.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Central remaining question.</span>  It remains to either show that the remaining points of <math id="S1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are achieved by primitive smooth covers, or (more dramatically) to show that there is some further obstruction to certain Tschirnhausen bundles being achieved for primitive smooth covers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p25.1" class="ltx_p">1.8.
<span id="S1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conventions.</span>  We work over <math id="S1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity, although the results readily extend in obvious ways to other fields. Henceforth, the phrase <span id="S1.p25.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cover of <math id="S1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> means a finite morphism <math id="S1.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from an irreducible curve <math id="S1.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the phrase <span id="S1.p25.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">smooth cover of <math id="S1.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> means further that <math id="S1.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.
Throughout <math id="S1.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> will refer to the degree of <math id="S1.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> will refer to the arithmetic genus of <math id="S1.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout this article, we fix a splitting</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\overset{\sim}{\longleftrightarrow}{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{1})\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{d-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟷</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\overset{\sim}{\longleftrightarrow}{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{1})\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p25.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S1.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are as described. It will be convenient to take the convention <math id="S1.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}=\oplus_{i=0}^{d-1}{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}=\oplus_{i=0}^{d-1}{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p26.1" class="ltx_p">We follow common notational conventions for Hirzebruch surfaces <math id="S1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{a}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{a}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="S1.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}\mathbb{F}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}\mathbb{F}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be the fiber class, let <math id="S1.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be the directrix (which is the unique section of negative self-intersection if
<math id="S1.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and has self-intersection <math id="S1.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a</annotation></semantics></math>). A section of <math id="S1.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> not intersecting the directrix has class <math id="S1.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=E+aF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=E+aF</annotation></semantics></math>. (See for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha</a>, §V.2]</cite> for standard facts about Hirzebruch surfaces.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p27.1" class="ltx_p">1.9.
<span id="S1.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgments.</span>  We thank Manjul Bhargava, Hendrik Lenstra, and Hannah Larson for many useful conversations. Melanie Wood’s work has also been an inspiration. The second author was supported by the NSF under grant number DMS2303211. We would also like to warmly thank Edoardo Ballico, Marc Coppens, Anand Deopurkar, Aaron Landesman, Hannah Larson, Hendrik Lenstra, Daniel Litt, Anand Patel, and Will Sawin for helpful comments on an earlier version of this draft. Finally, the referee’s careful reading significantly improved the manuscript.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Warm-up: Low degree (<math id="S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>) and low genus (<math id="S2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+1\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+1\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>)</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">2.1.
<span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The trivial cases <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>  The rank and degree of the bundle <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> determine the bundle for low-rank bundles. We quickly observe that for <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(g+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(g+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">2.2.
<span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The straightforward case <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> 

Before getting to the truly enlightening case of <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> case. Consider a degree <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> from a genus <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curve. Then <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}\geq e_{1}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}\geq e_{1}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}+e_{2}=g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}+e_{2}=g+2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=e_{2}-e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=e_{2}-e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for convenience. The canonical embedding
<math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}{\mathscr{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}{\mathscr{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> embeds <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hirzebruch surface
<math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{m}\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. A smooth irreducible trisection of <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the class <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3D+kF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3D+kF</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and a short calculation shows that such a curve has genus <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3m-2+2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3m-2+2k</annotation></semantics></math>. Now for <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to be such a curve, we must have <math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3m-2+2k=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3m-2+2k=g</annotation></semantics></math>, from which
<math id="S2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3(e_{2}-e_{1})-2+2k=e_{1}+e_{2}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3(e_{2}-e_{1})-2+2k=e_{1}+e_{2}-2</annotation></semantics></math>, from which <math id="S2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2e_{1}-e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2e_{1}-e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2e_{1}\geq e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2e_{1}\geq e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude that for
trigonal curves, we have <math id="S2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}+e_{2}=g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}+e_{2}=g+2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2e_{1}\geq e_{2}\geq e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2e_{1}\geq e_{2}\geq e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. (This is clearly <math id="S2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).) By choosing general curves in the appropriate linear system
<math id="S2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{F}_{m}}(3D+kF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{F}_{m}}(3D+kF)</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that there is indeed a trigonal cover
<math id="S2.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for each of these values of <math id="S2.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We have thus completely answered the Central Question in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We see that the scrollar invariants, suitably normalized, are precisely those that lie on the line segment joining <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/2,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/2,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/3,2/3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/3,2/3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">2.3.
<span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aside.</span>  The natural follow-up question is: as <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, how <em id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">often</em> do various points on this line segment occur from smooth curves. In §<a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we define a density function <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> from the aforementioned line segment to the real numbers, which quantifies the “likelihood” that a curve lands “near” a given point on the line segment. It turns out that the curves are not “equidistributed” on this line segment as <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently, <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a constant function, but indeed is a nontrivial linear function on the line segment).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We further conjecture that this fits into a more general framework (see §<a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> for more details). We define an arithmetic analogue <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math> capturing information about the shapes of rings of integers in cubic fields, as a lattice. Moreover, we define (conjecturally) a measure <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{gr}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{gr}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> on the line segment taking values in the Grothendieck ring of stacks, appropriately localized. We then define a (conjectural) function <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{gr}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{gr}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>, also taking values in the (appropriately completed) Grothendieck ring of stacks, which captures the point masses of this measure. We conjecture that <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dim\circ\rho^{gr}_{ref}=\rho^{geo}=\rho^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\circ\rho^{gr}_{ref}=\rho^{geo}=\rho^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">2.4.
<span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low genus.</span>  If the genus is low compared to the degree, the Tschirnhausen bundle is forced to be balanced (“as close to equal as possible given that they have to be integers”), as is made precise by the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">2.5.
<span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition. —</span>   <span id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq\frac{d-1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq\frac{d-1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-1)^{\oplus(d-1-g)}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-2)^{\oplus g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-1)^{\oplus(d-1-g)}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-2)^{\oplus g}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The proof showcases an idea, illustrated in the following lemma, that will be used in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. There are a number of essentially analogous lemmas in the arithmetic case; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR</a>, Lem. 6.4]</cite> or the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">BhSTTTZ</a>, Thm. 1.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">2.6.
<span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma. —</span>   <span id="S2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible curve, and <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a degree <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> cover. Then (following the notation of (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)) <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}+e_{d-1-i}\geq e_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}+e_{d-1-i}\geq e_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof. </span>Choose a point <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty\in\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty\in\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and choose a coordinate <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}=\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}=\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., an isomorphism <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{\infty\}\cong\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{C}[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{\infty\}\cong\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{C}[t]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the splitting <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{1})\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{1})\oplus\cdots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(-e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math>
induces a splitting of the <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}[t]</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra
<math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mathbb{A}^{1},\pi_{*}({\mathscr{O}}_{C}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mathbb{A}^{1},\pi_{*}({\mathscr{O}}_{C}))</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mathbb{A}^{1},\pi_{*}({\mathscr{O}}_{C}))=\mathbb{C}[t]\oplus\mathbb{C}[t]x_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathbb{C}[t]x_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mathbb{A}^{1},\pi_{*}({\mathscr{O}}_{C}))=\mathbb{C}[t]\oplus\mathbb{C}[t]x_{1}\oplus\cdots\oplus\mathbb{C}[t]x_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(as a <math id="S2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}[t]</annotation></semantics></math>-module); here we have chosen a generator <math id="S2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th summand of
<math id="S2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mathbb{A}^{1},\pi_{*}({\mathscr{O}}_{C}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mathbb{A}^{1},\pi_{*}({\mathscr{O}}_{C}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now <math id="S2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S2.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are a basis for <math id="S2.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(C)</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S2.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-module (where <math id="S2.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the function field) and the restriction of the multiplication in the <math id="S2.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\otimes\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\otimes\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\rightarrow\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">is simply multiplication <math id="S2.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(C)\otimes_{K(\mathbb{P}^{1})}K(C)\rightarrow K(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(C)\otimes_{K(\mathbb{P}^{1})}K(C)\rightarrow K(C)</annotation></semantics></math> in the field. Let <math id="S2.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S2.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrix given by taking the field multiplication map and projecting to the last coordinate. Observe that <math id="S2.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible because it represents a perfect pairing. Indeed, given any nonzero <math id="S2.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}\in K(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}\in K(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S2.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\in K(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\in K(C)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}v_{2}=x_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}v_{2}=x_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>; simply take <math id="S2.p10.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}=x_{d-1}/v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}=x_{d-1}/v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Now notice that for every <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq a,b&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq a,b&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, the summand corresponding to</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{a})\otimes{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{b})\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{d-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{a})\otimes{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{b})\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">is an element of <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{a}+e_{b}-e_{d-1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{a}+e_{b}-e_{d-1}))</annotation></semantics></math>, and is thus <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{a}+e_{b}&lt;e_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{a}+e_{b}&lt;e_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. But if <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}+e_{d-1-i}&lt;e_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}+e_{d-1-i}&lt;e_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then for <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\leq d-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\leq d-i</annotation></semantics></math>, the summand (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2. Warm-up: Low degree (
        
          
            ≤
            d
            3
          
        
      ) and low genus (
        
          
            ≤
            
              +
              
                ⁢
                2
                g
              
              1
            
            d
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is zero, so <math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be invertible, as it has an <math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i+1)\times(d-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i+1)\times(d-i)</annotation></semantics></math> submatrix that is zero.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">2.7.
<span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S2" title="2. Warm-up: Low degree (
        
          
            ≤
            d
            3
          
        
      ) and low genus (
        
          
            ≤
            
              +
              
                ⁢
                2
                g
              
              1
            
            d
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span> 
Recall that <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq e_{1}\leq\cdots\leq e_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq e_{1}\leq\cdots\leq e_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum e_{i}=d-1+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum e_{i}=d-1+g</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the result is then immediate, so assume <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced, then <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}=\cdots=e_{d-g-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}=\cdots=e_{d-g-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{d-g}=\cdots=e_{d-1}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{d-g}=\cdots=e_{d-1}=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
A quick thought will show that if <math id="S2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> balanced, then <math id="S2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{d-1}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{d-1}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{d-g}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{d-g}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. But then <math id="S2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{g-1}\leq e_{d-g}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{g-1}\leq e_{d-g}=1</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S2.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{g-1}+e_{d-g}&lt;e_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{g-1}+e_{d-g}&lt;e_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting Lemma <a href="#S2" title="2. Warm-up: Low degree (
        
          
            ≤
            d
            3
          
        
      ) and low genus (
        
          
            ≤
            
              +
              
                ⁢
                2
                g
              
              1
            
            d
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Key Example: Quartic covers (<math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>)</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to the case <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">3.1.
<span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem. —</span>   <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The possible scrollar invariants of tetragonal covers are precisely those that, when suitably scaled, are contained in the closed triangle shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, minus the point <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1/2,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1/2,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Every such triple is realized by a smooth bi-hyperelliptic cover. Moreover, the possible scrollar invariants of primitive tetragonal covers are those that, when suitably scaled, lie in the closed polytope <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2410.22531v4/triangle2.jpeg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="329" height="200" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Scrollar invariants of tetragonal covers (showing points <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3})\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3})\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>): the big triangle is <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, the shaded quadrilateral is <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the small triangle minus its intersection with <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily corresponds to imprimitive covers. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">3.2.
<span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">History.</span>  The case of quartic
covers has a long and rich history. We cannot hope to describe the
highlights in a short paragraph, but we mention a few relevant results.
We point out in particular <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CanL</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pa</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ve2</a>]</cite> for constraints on scrollar invariants of tetragonal covers. Also see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">CoM2</a>]</cite> for linear series on tetragonal covers (and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">CoM1</a>]</cite> for arbitrary degree);
and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">CasDC</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas2</a>]</cite> on degree <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> covers of more general varieties. Finally, strikingly: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">KO</a>]</cite> proves that every scrollar invariant
that (suitably scaled, and with <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) lies in <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is achieved by a tetragonal cover, but the covers described there are all <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">imprimitive</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">3.3.
<span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Observations.</span>  Before proving Theorem <a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we make some observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Any tetragonal cover <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{2})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}={\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{2})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}&gt;2e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}&gt;2e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily is not primitive; we will see that it factors through a hyperelliptic cover (and such curves have Galois/monodromy group smaller than <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the generic such curve has monodromy group <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Some of the vertices of <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to curves with (other) recognizable geometric meaning. For example, the generic genus <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> tetragonal curve has balanced scrollar invariants, so when <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\mid g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\mid g</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}\cong{\mathscr{O}}(\frac{1}{3}(g+3))^{\oplus 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}\cong{\mathscr{O}}(\frac{1}{3}(g+3))^{\oplus 3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the vertex <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/3,1/3,1/3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/3,1/3,1/3)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to “generic covers”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Second, curves <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of class <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4D</annotation></semantics></math> on the Hirzebruch surface <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> have genus <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6n-3</annotation></semantics></math>. By embedding <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (interpreted as a
<math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) as a conic bundle in the corresponding <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find curves
embedded in <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}({\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(n)\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(2n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}({\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(n)\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(2n))</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Suitably normalizing, this
corresponds to <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{e}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{e}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{e}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{e}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{e}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{e}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in arithmetic progression; this gives the vertex <math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/6,1/3,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/6,1/3,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. (These are the “least
generic” scrollar invariants for primitive covers.) Conversely, curves lying at the vertex <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/6,1/3,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/6,1/3,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely those of class <math id="S3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4D</annotation></semantics></math> on a Hirzebruch surface. These might be called monogenic covers, as they lie in <math id="S3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles over <math id="S3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Another reason for the term “monogenic” is that in this case, <math id="S3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S3.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2410.22531v4/monogenic.jpeg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="375" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Monogenic covers: <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3})=(1/6,1/3,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3})=(1/6,1/3,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The vertex <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/4,1/4,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/4,1/4,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds similarly to curves in <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles over <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The curves lying on this vertex are precisely those that do not intersect the Hirzebruch surface <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Proj({\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{2}))=v(x_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Proj({\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{2}))=v(x_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> in their embedding into <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}({\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}({\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The vertex <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/5,2/5,2/5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/5,2/5,2/5)</annotation></semantics></math> is harder to describe. These curves have the property that there exists a Hirzebruch surface <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{e_{3}-e_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{e_{3}-e_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the vanishing of the linear equation <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ax_{2}+bx_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ax_{2}+bx_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are the coordinates of <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}({\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}({\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math>. The defining linear equation is obtained as follows: there is a distinguished conic in the conic bundle, and it contains the line <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x_{2},x_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x_{2},x_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. The tangent space to that line forms the Hirzebruch surface <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{e_{3}-e_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{e_{3}-e_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Algebraically, if <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}^{\vee}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{2})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{3})={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2k)\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}^{\vee}={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{1})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{2})\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{3})={\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2k)\oplus{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2k)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.p9.m10.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="20.08" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-7.610350076103501px" version="1.1" viewBox="9.59 7.61 144.81 20.08" width="144.81"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 9.590762275473052 20.082863564476096) translate(0,3.46) translate(9.59,0)"><g transform="translate(-9.59,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.p9.m10.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{C\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{C\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(9.59,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 6.92 A 3.46 3.46 180 0 1 0 0" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 9.590762275473052 -0.27673578005007266 L 9.590762275473052 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(42.8,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 9.590762275473052 -0.27673578005007266 L 42.79956260756106 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 42.79956260756106 -0.27673578005007266 L 42.79956260756106 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(42.8,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.95em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="26.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.p9.m10.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\mathbb{P}{\mathscr{E}}^{\vee}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\mathbb{P}{\mathscr{E}}^{\vee}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 78.08397630116447 -0.27673578005007266 L 78.08397630116447 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(84.46,0) translate(0,6.24) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.09)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.p9.m10.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(111.29,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 78.08397630116447 -0.27673578005007266 L 111.29275551966583 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 111.29275551966583 -0.27673578005007266 L 111.29275551966583 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(111.29,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.13em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="15.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.p9.m10.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> with resolution</p>
