                    1
                  
                
              
            
          
        
       is not preserved under generization (
        
          d
          &gt;
          4
          ,
          g
          ≫
          
            d
          
          1
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">7.2.
<span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma. —</span>    Let <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a cover of degree <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> with scrollar invariants <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},\dots,e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}&gt;2e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}&gt;2e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{3}\leq e_{1}+e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{3}\leq e_{1}+e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof. </span>As in §<a href="#S4" title="4. Scrollar invariants of primitive covers lie in the polytope 
        
          
            
            P
            d ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, choose a coordinate <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}=\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}=\mathbb{P}^{1}\setminus\{\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a splitting</p>
<table id="S7.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\simeq{\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{d-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\simeq{\mathscr{O}}\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus{\mathscr{O}}(-e_{d-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=K(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=K(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=K(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=K(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. As in <a href="#S4" title="4. Scrollar invariants of primitive covers lie in the polytope 
        
          
            
            P
            d ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, this splitting gives rise to a basis <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,x_{1},\dots,x_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,x_{1},\dots,x_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for the vector space <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-subvector space spanned by <math id="S7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}&gt;2e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}&gt;2e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AA\subseteq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AA\subseteq A</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a quadratic field extension of <math id="S7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Now let <math id="S7.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S7.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-subvector space spanned by <math id="S7.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,x_{1},x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,x_{1},x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S7.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AB</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S7.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space and <math id="S7.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{K}A=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{K}A=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the quantity <math id="S7.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{K}AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{K}AB</annotation></semantics></math> must be even. Therefore, <math id="S7.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{K}AB\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{K}AB\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently there exists some integer <math id="S7.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}\leq e_{1}+e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}\leq e_{1}+e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">7.3.
<span id="S7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition. —</span>   <span id="S7.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix an integer <math id="S7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of length <math id="S7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math> nondecreasing concave sequences <math id="S7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> starting with <math id="S7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}=a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}=2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}=2a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{3}=3a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{3}=3a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{4}\geq 3a+5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{4}\geq 3a+5</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S7.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>). Let <math id="S7.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\{\sum e_{i}:(e_{i})\in S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\{\sum e_{i}:(e_{i})\in S\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{+}\setminus T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{+}\setminus T</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof. </span>For any <math id="S7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-2)a+5\leq e_{d-1}\leq(d-1)a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-2)a+5\leq e_{d-1}\leq(d-1)a</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence <math id="S7.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1}=a,e_{2}=2a,\dots,e_{d-2}=(d-2)a,e_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1}=a,e_{2}=2a,\dots,e_{d-2}=(d-2)a,e_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S7.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus for all <math id="S7.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, the interval <math id="S7.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[f(a),g(a)]\subset\mathbb{Z}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[f(a),g(a)]\subset\mathbb{Z}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S7.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(a)=a+\cdots+(d-2)a+((d-2)a+5)=\binom{d-1}{2}a+((d-2)a+5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(a)=a+\cdots+(d-2)a+((d-2)a+5)=\binom{d-1}{2}a+((d-2)a+5)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(a)=a+\cdots+(d-2)a+(d-1)a=\binom{d-1}{2}a+(d-1)a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(a)=a+\cdots+(d-2)a+(d-1)a=\binom{d-1}{2}a+(d-1)a</annotation></semantics></math>. But it is easy to check that (i) <math id="S7.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(a+1)&lt;g(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(a+1)&lt;g(a)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p8.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a\gg_{d}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\gg_{d}1</annotation></semantics></math> (as <math id="S7.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(a)-f(a+1)=a-h(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(a)-f(a+1)=a-h(d)</annotation></semantics></math> for appropriate <math id="S7.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(d)</annotation></semantics></math>), and (ii) <math id="S7.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(a)\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(a)\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, so these intervals cover all but finitely many of the positive integers. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p">7.4.
<span id="S7.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark: the set of Tschirnhausen bundles of primitive covers is not preserved under generization (<math id="S7.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g\gg_{d}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg_{d}1</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> 

Let <math id="S7.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}ur_{d,g}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the open substack of primitive covers, and let <math id="S7.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}^{p}:\mathcal{H}ur^{p}_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Tsch</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}^{p}:\mathcal{H}ur^{p}_{d,g}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}un_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the restriction. We now show that when <math id="S7.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p9.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g\gg_{d}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg_{d}1</annotation></semantics></math>, the image of <math id="S7.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Tsch}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Tsch</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tsch}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is not preserved under generization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, when <math id="S7.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>, take any nondecreasing sequence <math id="S7.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}=a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}=2a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}=2a</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+2\leq e_{3}\leq 3a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+2\leq e_{3}\leq 3a</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S7.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{3}\leq e_{1}+e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{3}\leq e_{1}+e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}\leq 2e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}\leq 2e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be realized as the scrollar invariants of a smooth primitive cover (Theorem <a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Let <math id="S7.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e^{\prime}_{1},e^{\prime}_{2},e^{\prime}_{3})=(e_{1},e_{2}+1,e_{3}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e^{\prime}_{1},e^{\prime}_{2},e^{\prime}_{3})=(e_{1},e_{2}+1,e_{3}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and note that the corresponding bundle does <em id="S7.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> arise as the Tschirnhausen bundle of a primitive quartic cover (Theorem <a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), but is indeed a generization of <math id="S7.p10.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{i=1}^{3}{\mathscr{O}}(e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{i=1}^{3}{\mathscr{O}}(e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. To conclude in this case it suffices to show that if <math id="S7.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of length <math id="S7.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> sequences <math id="S7.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> as above, then the set <math id="S7.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{+}\setminus\{\sum e_{i}:(e_{i})\in S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{+}\setminus\{\sum e_{i}:(e_{i})\in S\}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. When <math id="S7.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, the proof of Proposition <a href="#S7" title="7. The image of 
        
          
            :
            Tsch
            
              →
              
                ⁢
                H
                u
                
                  
                  r
                  
                    
                      
                      
                      
                    
                    
                      d
                      ,
                      g
                    
                  
                
              
              
                ⁢
                B
                u
                
                  
                  n
                  
                    
                    P
                    1
                  
                
              
            
          
        
       is not preserved under generization (
        
          d
          &gt;
          4
          ,
          g
          ≫
          
            d
          
          1
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> already shows that there exists a smooth <em id="S7.p10.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">primitive</em> cover whose Tschirnhausen bundle has a generization which is not the Tschirnhausen bundle of any smooth cover.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Motivation and analogy from number theory</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a particularly strong analogy to this question in number theory. The analogy to <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the analogy to the curve <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the ring of integers <math id="S8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> in a degree <math id="S8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> extension <math id="S8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">He</a>, §7]</cite> for more details on the arithmetic side.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our arguments in this paper extend readily to this arithmetic setting, but do not yield the same strength of results (number fields whose ”shape” is in this ”concave region”), because of one missing step: the need for a sieving result to ensure that there is a maximal order in a number field, which is strictly harder than the geometric analogue of Bertini’s Theorem (a blunt dimensional statement).</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">8.1.
<span id="S8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Extension: Density functions on the polytopes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now make precise the connection we predict between the geometry of degree <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> number fields, and the geometry of degree <math id="S8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="S8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a positive integer <math id="S8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">8.2.
<span id="S8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Definition.</span>  

For each open ball <math id="S8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S8.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{geo}(B,g)=\frac{1}{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}\dim\left(\bigcup_{\vec{e}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d-1}:\vec{e}/(d+g-1)\in B}\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{geo}(B,g)=\frac{1}{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}\dim\left(\bigcup_{\vec{e}\in\mathbb{Z}^{d-1}:\vec{e}/(d+g-1)\in B}\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of genus <math id="S8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="S8.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with scrollar invariants <math id="S8.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. (The dimension of a finite disjoint union is the maximum of the dimensions, and the dimension of the empty set is <math id="S8.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now define the arithmetic analogue of <math id="S8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{geo}(B,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{geo}(B,g)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a degree <math id="S8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> number field <math id="S8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0},\dots,\lambda_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0},\dots,\lambda_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the successive minima with respect to the following quadratic form: embed <math id="S8.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\xhookrightarrow{}\mathbb{C}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mover accent="true"><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">↪</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\xhookrightarrow{}\mathbb{C}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and take <math id="S8.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{d}</annotation></semantics></math> times the standard Hermitian quadratic form on <math id="S8.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Or even better: consider <math id="S8.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> as a metrized line bundle on <math id="S8.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}({\mathscr{O}}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}({\mathscr{O}}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>. Pushing forward via the degree <math id="S8.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="S8.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}({\mathscr{O}}_{K})\rightarrow\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}({\mathscr{O}}_{K})\rightarrow\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> gives a metrized vector bundle on <math id="S8.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S8.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\lambda_{0},\dots,\log\lambda_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\lambda_{0},\dots,\log\lambda_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are simply the slopes of this metrized vector bundle.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\prod_{i=1}^{d-1}\lambda_{i}\asymp_{d}\sqrt{\lvert\Disc(K)\rvert}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≍</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Disc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i=1}^{d-1}\lambda_{i}\asymp_{d}\sqrt{\lvert\Disc(K)\rvert}</annotation></semantics></math> by the AM-GM inequality and Minkowski’s second theorem respectively. The quantity <math id="S8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\lvert\Disc K\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Disc</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\lvert\Disc K\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> is analogous to <math id="S8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> and the quantities <math id="S8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are analogous to the scrollar invariants <math id="S8.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">8.3.
<span id="S8.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Definition.</span>  
For each open ball <math id="S8.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathbb{R}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S8.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{arith}(B,X)=\frac{\log\#\{K\;\mid\;\lvert\Disc(K)\rvert\in[X,2X],\;\log_{\sqrt{X}}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{d-1})\in B\}}{\log\#\{K\;\mid\;\lvert\Disc(K)\rvert\in[X,2X]\}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>#</mi><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">∣</mo><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Disc</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msqrt><mi>X</mi></msqrt></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>K</mi><mo fence="true">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Disc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{arith}(B,X)=\frac{\log\#\{K\;\mid\;\lvert\Disc(K)\rvert\in[X,2X],\;\log_{\sqrt{X}}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{d-1})\in B\}}{\log\#\{K\;\mid\;\lvert\Disc(K)\rvert\in[X,2X]\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p8.2" class="ltx_p">(By definition, we take <math id="S8.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(0)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(0)=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, just as the dimension of the empty set is taken to be <math id="S8.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Definition <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi=\{(\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1})\in\mathbb{R}^{d-1}:\sum\overline{e}_{i}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi=\{(\overline{e}_{1},\dots,\overline{e}_{d-1})\in\mathbb{R}^{d-1}:\sum\overline{e}_{i}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the hyperplane whose coordinates sum to <math id="S8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p10.1" class="ltx_p">8.4.
<span id="S8.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture. —</span> 

<span id="S8.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, both <math id="S8.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B,g)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{X\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{arith}(B,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{X\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{arith}(B,X)</annotation></semantics></math> exist, are equal, and depend only on the intersection of <math id="S8.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cap\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cap\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> and not on <math id="S8.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Conjecture <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is trivially true in the case <math id="S8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>. We give a proof in the case <math id="S8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2410.22531v4/cubicoffline.png" id="S8.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" width="461" height="462" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>An artist’s depiction of the points <math id="S8.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\log_{\sqrt{X}}\lambda_{1},\log_{\sqrt{X}}\lambda_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><msqrt><mi>X</mi></msqrt></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><msqrt><mi>X</mi></msqrt></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\log_{\sqrt{X}}\lambda_{1},\log_{\sqrt{X}}\lambda_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> as we range over cubic fields with absolute discriminant in the interval <math id="S8.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[X,2X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[X,2X]</annotation></semantics></math>. Note how the points cluster close to the line segment, but are not necessarily contained on it. Moreover, the points are concentrated near the point <math id="S8.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/2,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/2,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and become more sparse as we move to the left hand side of the line segment. Beyond the left hand side of the line segment, the points abruptly end.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S8.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p12.1" class="ltx_p">8.5.
<span id="S8.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition. —</span> 

<span id="S8.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conjecture <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is true when <math id="S8.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof. </span>The locus <math id="S8.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty precisely when <math id="S8.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}+e_{2}=g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}+e_{2}=g+2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}\leq e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}\leq e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S8.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}\leq 2e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}\leq 2e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the dimension of <math id="S8.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},g}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S8.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\operatorname{Sym}^{3}({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))\otimes{\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e})^{\vee})-h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\End({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e})))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\operatorname{Sym}^{3}({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))\otimes{\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e})^{\vee})-h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\End({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p13.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the dimension of <math id="S8.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{3,g}=2g+5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{3,g}=2g+5</annotation></semantics></math>. So, if <math id="S8.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> nontrivially intersects the closed line segment from <math id="S8.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/3,2/3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/3,2/3)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/2,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/2,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S8.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B,g)=\sup\left\{1-\frac{\overline{e}_{2}-\overline{e}_{1}}{2}:(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2})\in B\cap\Xi\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B,g)=\sup\left\{1-\frac{\overline{e}_{2}-\overline{e}_{1}}{2}:(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2})\in B\cap\Xi\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p13.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S8.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cap\Xi=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cap\Xi=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S8.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B,g)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B,g)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The equivalent computation in the arithmetic case can essentially be found in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ve3</a>, Thm. 1.6]</cite>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p14.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S8.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>, the difficulty of sieving out maximal orders is already too hard in the arithmetic setting. For case of not necessarily maximal orders when <math id="S8.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ve3</a>]</cite> for a description of <math id="S8.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math>. In these cases, the nonmaximal version of <math id="S8.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math> is described as the maximum of a continuous piecewise linear function supported on a polytope; in these known cases, this matches the version of <math id="S8.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{geo}(B,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{geo}(B,g)</annotation></semantics></math> including singular curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{geo}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{geo}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{arith}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{arith}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding limits. One might be tempted to ask: how does the density of curves or number fields concentrate on specific points of <math id="S8.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>? The following conjectural density function is meant to capture that information. For a point <math id="S8.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> define</p>
<table id="S8.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}(x)=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B_{i}(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}(x)=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}(B_{i}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p15.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{i}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{i}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of open balls all containing <math id="S8.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and with radius converging to <math id="S8.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S8.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math> similarly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p16.1" class="ltx_p">8.6.
<span id="S8.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture. —</span> 

<span id="S8.p16.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The quantities <math id="S8.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{arith}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{arith}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> exist and are equal. They are supported on a finite union of rational polytopes, are piecewise linear, and are continuous on their support.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p17.1" class="ltx_p">8.7.
<span id="S8.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary. —</span>  <span id="S8.p17.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S8.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3,4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3,4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, Conjecture <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is true.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof. </span>The case <math id="S8.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the explicit description of <math id="S8.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{geo}(B_{i}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{geo}(B_{i}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> given in Proposition <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The case <math id="S8.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Theorem <a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (see Proposition <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). The case <math id="S8.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5</annotation></semantics></math> can similarly be extracted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">FV</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p19.1" class="ltx_p">8.8.
<span id="S8.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Further extension: refined density functions taking into account “all” scrollar invariants.</span> 
As we saw in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, a quartic cover has three scrollar invariants <math id="S8.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and an associated trigonal cover with scrollar invariants <math id="S8.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property that <math id="S8.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}+e_{2}+e_{3}=f_{1}+f_{2}=g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}+e_{2}+e_{3}=f_{1}+f_{2}=g+3</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, we can consider <em id="S8.p19.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">refined</em> density functions which take into account the extra data of <math id="S8.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p20" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p20.1" class="ltx_p">8.9.
<span id="S8.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Definition.</span>   <span id="S8.p20.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each open ball <math id="S8.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathbb{R}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathbb{R}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{ref}^{geo}(B,g)=\frac{1}{\dim\mathcal{H}_{4,g}}\dim\left(\bigcup_{(\vec{e},\vec{f})\in\mathbb{Z}^{5}:(\vec{e},\vec{f})/(g+3)\in B}\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}^{geo}(B,g)=\frac{1}{\dim\mathcal{H}_{4,g}}\dim\left(\bigcup_{(\vec{e},\vec{f})\in\mathbb{Z}^{5}:(\vec{e},\vec{f})/(g+3)\in B}\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p20.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p20.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S8.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of genus <math id="S8.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> degree <math id="S8.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="S8.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with scrollar invariants <math id="S8.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. (As before, the dimension of a finite disjoint union is the maximum of the dimensions, and the dimension of the empty set is <math id="S8.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.)
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, one can define <math id="S8.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math> (in this case, we replace the data of <math id="S8.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with the successive minima of the cubic resolvent ring). As before, we obtain conjectural density functions <math id="S8.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using our work in §<a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we compute <math id="S8.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> and give the formula below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p22.1" class="ltx_p">8.10.
<span id="S8.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition. —</span>  <span id="S8.p22.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
In the notation of Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the function <math id="S8.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on the union of <math id="S8.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}^{\prime}\cap\{(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{5}:\overline{f}_{1}=2\overline{e}_{1}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}^{\prime}\cap\{(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{5}:\overline{f}_{1}=2\overline{e}_{1}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On this polytope, we have</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2})=1-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{ijk}\max\{0,\overline{f}_{k}-\overline{e}_{i}-\overline{e}_{j}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2})=1-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{ijk}\max\{0,\overline{f}_{k}-\overline{e}_{i}-\overline{e}_{j}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p22.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p22.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, the projection <math id="S8.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on the polytope <math id="S8.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{4}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{4}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and is given by the formula</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3})=\sup\{\rho_{ref}^{geo}(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2}):\overline{f}_{1}+\overline{f}_{2}=1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3})=\sup\{\rho_{ref}^{geo}(\overline{e}_{1},\overline{e}_{2},\overline{e}_{3};\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2}):\overline{f}_{1}+\overline{f}_{2}=1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.p23" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof. </span>Theorem <a href="#S3" title="3. Key Example: Quartic covers (
        
          
            =
            d
            4
          
        
      ) ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> classifies which <math id="S8.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3};f_{1},f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3};f_{1},f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> arise from smooth quartic covers. When <math id="S8.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty, its dimension is given by</p>
<table id="S8.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\operatorname{Sym}^{2}{\mathscr{E}}\otimes{\mathscr{F}}^{\vee})-h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\End({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e})))-h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\End({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{f})))+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℱ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\operatorname{Sym}^{2}{\mathscr{E}}\otimes{\mathscr{F}}^{\vee})-h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\End({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e})))-h^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\End({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{f})))+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p23.2" class="ltx_p">After expanding the expression above into a piecewise linear function in <math id="S8.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and then taking appropriate limits, one obtains the claimed value for <math id="S8.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>. An analogous argument gives the claimed value for <math id="S8.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\rm{geo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>geo</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\rm{geo}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We leave the details to the reader.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p24.1" class="ltx_p">We may now make a conjecture analogous to Conjecture <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p25.1" class="ltx_p">8.11.
<span id="S8.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture. —</span> 

<span id="S8.p25.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The quantities <math id="S8.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{arith}_{ref}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{arith}_{ref}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> exist and are equal. They satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}=\rho_{ref}^{arith}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}=\rho_{ref}^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p25.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p25.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and are supported on a finite union of rational polytopes, piecewise linear, and continuous on their support.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p26.1" class="ltx_p">8.12.
<span id="S8.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Final remarks: refined density functions in all degrees.</span> 
For <math id="S8.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5</annotation></semantics></math>, it is natural to make a conjecture analogous to Conjecture <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Canonically associated to a smooth degree <math id="S8.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> cover is a degree <math id="S8.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> cover called its sextic resolvent, and one should replace the data of <math id="S8.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> in the quartic case with the data of the scrollar invariants of the sextic resolvent. Indeed from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">FV</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite> one may compute an explicit description of <math id="S8.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> for quintic covers. In this case, the density function <math id="S8.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on a union of two polytopes, is piecewise linear, and is continuous on its support.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p27.1" class="ltx_p">For general degree <math id="S8.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, the work of Casnati and Ekedahl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">CasE</a>, Thm. 1.3]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas1</a>]</cite>) shows that a smooth degree <math id="S8.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="S8.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> canonically embeds in the projectivization of the Tschirnhausen bundle. The minimal resolution of <math id="S8.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> inside the <math id="S8.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle is of the form</p>
<table id="S8.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\mathcal{N}_{d-2}(-d)\rightarrow\mathcal{N}_{d-3}(-d+2)\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow\mathcal{N}_{1}(-2)\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\mathcal{N}_{d-2}(-d)\rightarrow\mathcal{N}_{d-3}(-d+2)\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow\mathcal{N}_{1}(-2)\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\rightarrow{\mathscr{O}}_{C}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p27.2" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S8.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{N}_{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{N}_{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> are vector bundles on <math id="S8.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> whose ranks are determined by <math id="S8.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and whose degrees are of the form <math id="S8.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}(d+g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}(d+g-1)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S8.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer depending only on <math id="S8.p27.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p27.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p28.1" class="ltx_p">In the case <math id="S8.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, the Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S8.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{E}}_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> determines every bundle in the minimal resolution. When <math id="S8.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, the Tschirnhausen bundle of the associated cubic resolvent cover (resp. sextic resolvent cover), along with the Tschirnhausen bundle of the curve itself, determines every bundle in the minimal resolution. In general, one may consider the sublocus of <math id="S8.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S8.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{N}_{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{N}_{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, and define a (conjectural) refined density function <math id="S8.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{geo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{geo}</annotation></semantics></math>. By using the tuning modules defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">WY</a>]</cite>, which give lattices analogous to the vector bundles <math id="S8.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, one may define a (conjectural) arithmetic refined density function <math id="S8.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{ref}^{arith}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{ref}^{arith}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is interesting to ask if the natural conjecture, analogous to Conjecture <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, is true in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p29.1" class="ltx_p">8.13.
<span id="S8.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Furthest extension: density functions valued in the Grothendieck ring.</span> 
There is a natural analogous question to consider in the Grothendieck ring of stacks, which we only state when <math id="S8.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume henceforth that <math id="S8.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">E</a>]</cite>,
let <math id="S8.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk</annotation></semantics></math> be the space obtained by taking the Grothendieck ring of varieties, inverting <math id="S8.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then completing with respect to the dimensional filtration. Then <math id="S8.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk</annotation></semantics></math> comes with a ”dimension function” <math id="S8.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim:\grstk\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>dim</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim:\grstk\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S8.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\dim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>dim</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\dim</annotation></semantics></math> is a valuation on <math id="S8.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p31.1" class="ltx_p">8.14.
<span id="S8.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Motivation.</span>  
The goal of this section will be to define (conjecturally) a function <math id="S8.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{gr}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{gr}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> taking values in <math id="S8.p31.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if <math id="S8.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{gr}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{gr}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined, then <math id="S8.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{gr}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{gr}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S8.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}_{ref}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}_{ref}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> (a real-valued function) plus negative-dimensional “correction terms”:</p>
<table id="S8.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{gr}_{ref}(x)=\rho^{geo}_{ref}(x)+Z(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{gr}_{ref}(x)=\rho^{geo}_{ref}(x)+Z(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p31.2" class="ltx_p">for some function <math id="S8.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(Z(x))&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(Z(x))&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S8.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. (We consider <math id="S8.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}_{ref}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}_{ref}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as taking values in <math id="S8.p31.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> via the natural map <math id="S8.p31.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p32.1" class="ltx_p">8.15.
<span id="S8.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Setup.</span>  
Note first that <math id="S8.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{geo}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{geo}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined using <em id="S8.p32.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quotients</em> of dimensions. To emulate this in <math id="S8.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk</annotation></semantics></math>, we will first partially define a logarithm with base <math id="S8.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> taking values in <math id="S8.p32.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and take quotients of the form</p>
<table id="S8.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\log_{\mathbb{L}}X}{\log_{\mathbb{L}}Y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\log_{\mathbb{L}}X}{\log_{\mathbb{L}}Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p32.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y\in\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y\in\grstk</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S8.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then it will follow from our definition of logarithm (Definition <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) that</p>
<table id="S8.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\log_{\mathbb{L}}X}{\log_{\mathbb{L}}Y}=\frac{\dim X}{\dim Y}+Z" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\log_{\mathbb{L}}X}{\log_{\mathbb{L}}Y}=\frac{\dim X}{\dim Y}+Z</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p32.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(Z)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(Z)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the quotient of the logarithms is the quotient of dimensions plus lower-dimensional terms, as desired. Conjecture <a href="#S8" title="8. Motivation and analogy from number theory ‣ Tschirnhausen bundles of covers of the projective line" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is about the convergence of <math id="S8.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X/\dim Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X/\dim Y</annotation></semantics></math>, in an appropriate limit; in this section we extend to conjecture the convergence of the <math id="S8.p32.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>-term.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p33" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p33.1" class="ltx_p">8.16.
<span id="S8.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Definition.</span>  <span id="S8.p33.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S8.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\grstk</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S8.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathbb{L}^{\dim X}\times(1+Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbb{L}^{\dim X}\times(1+Y)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S8.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Y&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Y&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.p33.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log_{\mathbb{L}}(X):=\dim X+\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{i+1}\frac{Y^{i}}{i}\in\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{\mathbb{L}}(X):=\dim X+\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{i+1}\frac{Y^{i}}{i}\in\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p34.1" class="ltx_p">As stated previously, to a degree <math id="S8.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cover there is a naturally associated pair <math id="S8.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{f}=(f_{1},f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{f}=(f_{1},f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, and to a degree <math id="S8.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> cover there is a naturally associated quintuple <math id="S8.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{f}=(f_{1},\dots,f_{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{f}=(f_{1},\dots,f_{5})</annotation></semantics></math>. For the sake of convention, we associate to a degree <math id="S8.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> cover the <em id="S8.p34.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empty</em> tuple <math id="S8.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{f}=()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{f}=()</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p35.1" class="ltx_p">8.17.
<span id="S8.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma. —</span>   <span id="S8.p35.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S8.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty, then <math id="S8.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}}\times(1+Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}}\times(1+Y)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S8.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\grstk</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(Y)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(Y)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S8.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}\times(1+Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}\times(1+Y)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S8.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\grstk</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(Y)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(Y)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S8.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]</annotation></semantics></math> are well-defined. Moreover <math id="S8.p35.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible in <math id="S8.p35.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p36" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof. </span>We begin by proving that <math id="S8.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]</annotation></semantics></math> have the specified form. Each <math id="S8.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> has some <math id="S8.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> as an open dense locus; therefore it suffices to prove the claim for <math id="S8.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas1</a>]</cite>, if <math id="S8.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty then</p>
<table id="S8.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{f}))]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{f}))]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p36.2" class="ltx_p">in the cases <math id="S8.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4,5</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, for some integer <math id="S8.p36.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and some element <math id="S8.p36.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\grstk</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p36.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(X)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(X)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. A short computation shows that for any vector bundle <math id="S8.p36.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S8.p36.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S8.p36.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))}(1+Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))}(1+Z)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S8.p36.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\grstk</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S8.p36.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Z&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Z&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The power series expansion</p>
<table id="S8.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{1+z}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^{n}z^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{1+z}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^{n}z^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p36.3" class="ltx_p">implies that <math id="S8.p36.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]^{-1}=\mathbb{L}^{-\dim\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))}\left(\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^{n}Z^{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>GL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>GL</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]^{-1}=\mathbb{L}^{-\dim\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))}\left(\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^{n}Z^{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting, we obtain</p>
<table id="S8.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]^{-1}=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times(1-Z+Z^{2}\dots)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]^{-1}=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times(1-Z+Z^{2}\dots)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]^{-1}\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{f}))]^{-1}=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times(1-Z_{e}+\dots)\times(1-Z_{f}+\dots)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{e}))]^{-1}\times[\GL({\mathscr{O}}(\vec{f}))]^{-1}=\mathbb{L}^{k}\times(1+X)\times(1-Z_{e}+\dots)\times(1-Z_{f}+\dots),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p36.4" class="ltx_p">respectively in the cases <math id="S8.p36.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p36.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4,5</annotation></semantics></math> as required.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p37.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove that <math id="S8.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible in <math id="S8.p37.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have shown that</p>
<table id="S8.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}\times(1+Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]=\mathbb{L}^{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}\times(1+Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p37.2" class="ltx_p">for some <math id="S8.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\grstk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\grstk</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(Y)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(Y)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition</p>
<table id="S8.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]=\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}+\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{i+1}\frac{Y^{i}}{i}=\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}\times(1+W)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]=\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}+\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{i+1}\frac{Y^{i}}{i}=\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}\times(1+W)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p37.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=(\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g})^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{i+1}\frac{Y^{i}}{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=(\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g})^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{i+1}\frac{Y^{i}}{i}</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="S8.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(W)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(W)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S8.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S8.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g})^{-1}(1+W)^{-1}=(\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g})^{-1}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^{n}W^{n}\in\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g})^{-1}(1+W)^{-1}=(\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g})^{-1}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^{n}W^{n}\in\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p37.4" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p38.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S8.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is an open ball in <math id="S8.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S8.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, a potential alternate definition of <math id="S8.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S8.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{ref,avg}^{geo}(B,g)=\frac{\avg\left\{\dim\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}:(\vec{e},\vec{f})/(d+g-1)\in B,\;\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\neq\emptyset\right\}}{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>avg</mi><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref,avg}^{geo}(B,g)=\frac{\avg\left\{\dim\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}:(\vec{e},\vec{f})/(d+g-1)\in B,\;\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\neq\emptyset\right\}}{\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p38.2" class="ltx_p">(By convention, we take <math id="S8.p38.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> be the average of the empty set.) Although <math id="S8.p38.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref,avg}^{geo}(B,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref,avg}^{geo}(B,g)</annotation></semantics></math> may not be equal to <math id="S8.p38.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{geo}_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{geo}_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>, a short computation shows that the limit <math id="S8.p38.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref,avg}^{geo}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref,avg}^{geo}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> still exists, and moreover</p>
<table id="S8.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{geo}_{ref}=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}_{ref}(B_{i}(x))=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}_{ref,avg}(B_{i}(x))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{geo}_{ref}=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}_{ref}(B_{i}(x))=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{geo}_{ref,avg}(B_{i}(x)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p38.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.p38.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is any set of open balls all containing <math id="S8.p38.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and with radius converging to <math id="S8.p38.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words the resulting density functions are equal. We make this equivalent alternate definition because it suggests a corresponding definition in the Grothendieck ring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p39.1" class="ltx_p">Thus motivated, we define</p>
<table id="S8.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B,g)=\frac{\avg\left\{{\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]:(\vec{e},\vec{f})/(d+g-1)\in B,\;\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\neq\emptyset}\right\}}{\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>avg</mi><mo>{</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝕃</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B,g)=\frac{\avg\left\{{\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}]:(\vec{e},\vec{f})/(d+g-1)\in B,\;\mathcal{H}_{\vec{e},\vec{f}}\neq\emptyset}\right\}}{\log_{\mathbb{L}}[\mathcal{H}_{d,g}]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p39.2" class="ltx_p">taking values in <math id="S8.p39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. We may then proceed to make an analogous conjecture:</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p40" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p40.1" class="ltx_p">8.18.
<span id="S8.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture (?). —</span> 

<span id="S8.p40.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S8.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="S8.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{g\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B,g)</annotation></semantics></math> converges in <math id="S8.p40.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Stck</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\grstk\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p41.1" class="ltx_p">There is little direct evidence for this conjecture (perhaps it should be a question), but because any counterexample to this conjecture would be very interesting, we feel it is worth making. As before, let <math id="S8.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding limit, and for a point <math id="S8.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S8.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively define</p>
<table id="S8.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{gr}_{ref}(x)=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B_{i}(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{gr}_{ref}(x)=\lim_{i\rightarrow\infty}\pi^{gr}_{ref,avg}(B_{i}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p41.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S8.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{i}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{i}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is any set of open balls all containing <math id="S8.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and with radius converging to <math id="S8.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p42.1" class="ltx_p">8.19.
<span id="S8.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture. —</span> 
 <span id="S8.p42.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="S8.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, the limit <math id="S8.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{gr}_{ref}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{gr}_{ref}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> exists.
</span></p>
</div>
</section>
